<!doctype html><html lang="en" class="js-disabled"><head><script>window.dataLayer=window.dataLayer||[];window.dataLayer.push({"asn":3462,"as_org":"HiNet Taiwan"});</script><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, minimum-scale=1"/><title>Measuring inequality: what is the Gini coefficient? | Our World in Data</title><meta name="description" content="The Gini coefficient is the most common way of measuring inequality. But what does it actually measure? And how does it differ from other measures of inequality?"/><link rel="canonical" href="https://ourworldindata.org/what-is-the-gini-coefficient"/><link rel="alternate" type="application/atom+xml" href="/atom.xml" title="Atom feed for Our World in Data"/><link rel="search" type="application/opensearchdescription+xml" href="/opensearch.xml" title="Our World in Data"/><link rel="archives" href="https://archive.ourworldindata.org/20261019-210306/what-is-the-gini-coefficient.html" title="Archived version of this chart as of 2026-10-19" data-archival-date="20261019-210306"/><link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="32x32"/><link rel="icon" href="/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"/><link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png"/><link rel="preload" href="/fonts/LatoLatin-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin="anonymous"/><meta property="og:url" content="https://ourworldindata.org/what-is-the-gini-coefficient"/><meta property="og:title" content="Measuring inequality: what is the Gini coefficient?"/><meta property="og:description" content="The Gini coefficient is the most common way of measuring inequality. But what does it actually measure? And how does it differ from other measures of inequality?"/><meta property="og:image" content="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/311c2eff-478c-44e3-d41b-271b651e7f00/public"/><meta property="og:site_name" content="Our World in Data"/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/><meta name="twitter:site" content="@OurWorldInData"/><meta name="twitter:creator" content="@OurWorldInData"/><meta name="twitter:title" content="Measuring inequality: what is the Gini coefficient?"/><meta name="twitter:description" content="The Gini coefficient is the most common way of measuring inequality. But what does it actually measure? And how does it differ from other measures of inequality?"/><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/311c2eff-478c-44e3-d41b-271b651e7f00/public"/><link rel="modulepreload" href="/assets/owid.mjs"/><link rel="preload" href="/assets/owid.css" as="style"/><meta name="citation_title" content="Measuring inequality: what is the Gini coefficient?"/><meta name="citation_fulltext_html_url" content="https://ourworldindata.org/what-is-the-gini-coefficient"/><meta name="citation_fulltext_world_readable" content=""/><meta name="citation_publication_date" content="2023/06/30"/><meta name="citation_journal_title" content="Our World in Data"/><meta name="citation_journal_abbrev" content="Our World in Data"/><meta name="citation_author" content="Joe Hasell"/><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/owid.css"/><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Measuring inequality: what is the Gini coefficient?","description":"The Gini coefficient is the most common way of measuring inequality. But what does it actually measure? And how does it differ from other measures of inequality?","image":["https://ourworldindata.org/cdn-cgi/imagedelivery/qLq-8BTgXU8yG0N6HnOy8g/311c2eff-478c-44e3-d41b-271b651e7f00/public"],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://ourworldindata.org/what-is-the-gini-coefficient"},"datePublished":"2023-06-30T07:18:00.000Z","author":[{"@type":"Person","name":"Joe Hasell","url":"https://ourworldindata.org/team/joe-hasell"}],"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Our World in Data","url":"https://ourworldindata.org","logo":"https://ourworldindata.org/owid-logo.png"}}</script><script>window._OWID_GDOC_PROPS = {"id":"17-2C6H3kPd7w9DxWT2RG2QuLP0Pzxvm4H2RVsWFWUzM","slug":"what-is-the-gini-coefficient","content":{"toc":[{"slug":"method-1-the-gini-tells-us-the-difference-we-expect-to-find-between-any-two-people-s-incomes-relative-to-the-mean","title":"Method 1: The Gini tells us the difference we expect to find between any two people’s incomes relative to the mean","supertitle":"","isSubheading":false},{"slug":"method-2-the-gini-tells-us-how-far-the-lorenz-curve-falls-from-perfect-equality","title":"Method 2: The Gini tells us how far the “Lorenz curve” falls from perfect equality","supertitle":"","isSubheading":false}],"body":[{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"The Gini coefficient, or Gini index, is the most commonly used measure of inequality. It was developed by Italian statistician Corrado Gini (1884–1965) and is named after him.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"It is typically used as a measure of ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"children":[{"text":"income","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-italic"},{"text":" inequality, but it can be used to measure the inequality of any distribution — such as the distribution of wealth or even life expectancy.","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"url":"#note-1","children":[{"children":[{"text":"1","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-superscript"}],"spanType":"span-ref"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"It measures inequality on a scale from 0 to 1, where higher values indicate higher inequality. This can sometimes be shown as a percentage from 0 to 100%, called the “Gini Index”.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"A value of 0 indicates perfect equality: everyone has the same income. A value of 1 indicates perfect inequality, where one person receives all the income, and everyone else receives nothing.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"url":"https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/gini-coefficient-wb?country=USA%7EFRA%7EIDN%7EURY&survey_comparability=no_spells","size":"wide","type":"chart","parseErrors":[]},{"text":[{"text":"How is the Gini coefficient calculated?","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"type":"heading","level":1,"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"There are two main ways of calculating the Gini coefficient. Both arrive at the same value, but they provide us with two different angles for understanding what it measures.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"text":[{"text":"Method 1: The Gini tells us the difference we expect to find between any two people’s incomes relative to the mean","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"type":"heading","level":2,"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"The first method can be illustrated with the following thought experiment.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"Imagine two people bumping into each other in the street at random. They compare their incomes and find out how rich one person is compared to the other. How big a gap would we ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"children":[{"text":"expect","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-italic"},{"text":" there to be?","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"This expected gap between two randomly chosen people is what the Gini coefficient measures. It is calculated by taking the average gap between all pairs of people.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"Where incomes are distributed equally, we expect the gap between two randomly selected people to be small. Where inequality is high, we expect the gap to be large.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"However, if measured in absolute terms, this will also depend on how rich or poor the population is generally. Where even the most well-off in society have a low income, the absolute gap between people’s incomes cannot be high. Conversely, where incomes are generally high, even very small relative differences between people's incomes can result in large absolute gaps.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"For this reason, the Gini coefficient expresses the expected absolute gap between people’s incomes ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"children":[{"text":"relative to the mean income","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-italic"},{"text":" in the population.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"In particular, it is calculated as the expected gap as a share of twice the mean income. Twice the mean income is the highest possible value for the average gap — a situation of perfect inequality, where one person has all the income and everyone else has none.","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"url":"#note-2","children":[{"children":[{"text":"2","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-superscript"}],"spanType":"span-ref"},{"text":" So in this case of maximum inequality, the Gini coefficient is 1.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"The lowest possible value for the average gap between all pairs of people is zero—a situation of perfect equality, where there are no gaps between any two people’s incomes because everyone earns the same. In this case, the Gini coefficient is 0.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"text":[{"text":"Method 2: The Gini tells us how far the “Lorenz curve” falls from perfect equality","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"type":"heading","level":2,"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"The figure illustrates a second visual definition of the Gini coefficient.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"The left panel shows the share of income received by each fifth of a hypothetical population. The right panel shows this data plotted cumulatively. This is known as a “Lorenz curve”.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"In a population where income is shared perfectly equally, the Lorenz curve would be a straight diagonal line: 10% of the population would earn 10% of the total income, 20% would earn 20% of the total income, and so on. This is shown in the chart as the “line of equality”.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"In the hypothetical population shown in the chart, though, incomes are not distributed equally. The bottom 60% of the population earns 30% of the total income.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"The Gini coefficient captures how far the Lorenz curve falls from the “line of equality” by comparing the areas A and B, as calculated in the following way:","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"Gini coefficient = A / (A + B)","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"The Lorenz curve is the “line of equality” where incomes are shared perfectly equally. Area A is 0, and hence so is the Gini coefficient. Where one person has all income and all others receive no income, the Lorenz curve will run along the bottom axis of the chart — the cumulative share of income is zero until the very last person. Area B will be zero, and the Gini coefficient will equal 1.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"size":"wide","type":"image","filename":"Gini-measure-schematic-1.png","hasOutline":true,"parseErrors":[]},{"text":[{"text":"Comparing measures: Does the Gini tell the same story as other inequality measures?","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"type":"heading","level":1,"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"A measure of inequality summarizes how spread out the distribution is — just like the standard deviation, which you may have learned about in school.","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"url":"#note-3","children":[{"children":[{"text":"3","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-superscript"}],"spanType":"span-ref"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"Implicit in such summary measures are judgments about what should count most when measuring inequality.","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"url":"#note-4","children":[{"children":[{"text":"4","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-superscript"}],"spanType":"span-ref"},{"text":" For example, compare two hypothetical populations. In one, the rich are ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"children":[{"text":"much","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-italic"},{"text":" richer than those in the middle, but the incomes of poorer people are only a little below those in the middle. In another, there is the opposite situation: the incomes of the rich are only a little above those in the middle, but the poor are ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"children":[{"text":"much","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-italic"},{"text":" poorer. In which population would you say inequality is highest? Your answer will depend on your personal judgments about how these gaps in different parts of the distribution compare in terms of their contribution to inequality overall. Such value judgments are implicitly built into the mathematical definition of an inequality measure.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"That is true of all inequality measures, and the Gini coefficient is no exception. One property of the Gini, compared to other inequality metrics, is that it is more sensitive to changes around the middle of the distribution than at the very top and bottom.","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"url":"#note-5","children":[{"children":[{"text":"5","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-superscript"}],"spanType":"span-ref"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"You can see this from the examples in the chart below. For four countries, it shows how the Gini compares to two other inequality measures: the share of income received by the richest 10% and 1%, respectively. To make more direct comparisons, the chart shows the relative change in each measure over time.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"aside","caption":[{"text":"You can see the actual values for each measure by unselecting “Relative change” in the chart.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"We see that modest relative changes in the Gini coefficient can be accompanied by much more substantial relative changes in the share of income received by the richest 1%. That is true where inequality is rising (as in the United States) ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"children":[{"text":"and","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-italic"},{"text":" where inequality is falling (as in Uruguay).","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"aside","caption":[{"text":"Change the countries and years selected using the “Add country or region” button and the time slider in the chart.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"In contrast, the Gini coefficient tracks the top 10% share of income much more closely. The Gini's lack of sensitivity to changes at the top of the distribution is mostly a matter of concern for the ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"children":[{"text":"very","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-italic"},{"text":" top.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]},{"name":"three-metrics-of-inequality-usa-fra-idn-ury","size":"wide","type":"narrative-chart","parseErrors":[]}],"refs":{"errors":[],"definitions":{"27160ceb815410ace8036cc7515d7b7b9700c8c3":{"id":"27160ceb815410ace8036cc7515d7b7b9700c8c3","index":4,"content":[{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"See Anthony B Atkinson, “On the Measurement of Inequality”. ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"children":[{"text":"Journal of Economic Theory","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-italic"},{"text":" 2, no. 3 (1970): 244–63. Available ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"url":"http://faculty.ucr.edu/~jorgea/econ261/atkinson_inequality.pdf","children":[{"text":"here","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-link"},{"text":".","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]}],"parseErrors":[]},"6cd7a31946de93795cc262faa66c3f7302a2abe6":{"id":"6cd7a31946de93795cc262faa66c3f7302a2abe6","index":0,"content":[{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"Generally, the Gini coefficient is not used to calculate inequality for distributions that include negative values. For example, the distribution of wealth will include many people with negative wealth — those whose debts are greater than their assets. The Gini coefficient can be calculated over such distributions, but this can result in values greater than 1 — making the metric difficult to interpret. When calculating a Gini coefficient on a distribution that includes negative values, these values are typically dropped or replaced with a value of zero.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]}],"parseErrors":[]},"af31d8e652292d35d19b4b87a5b12ac63ae3c3bb":{"id":"af31d8e652292d35d19b4b87a5b12ac63ae3c3bb","index":2,"content":[{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"The standard deviation too is sometimes used as a measure of income inequality — though, to make it a measure of relative inequality, it is usually first used by dividing by the mean, similar to the Gini. This measure is known as the Coefficient of Variation.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]}],"parseErrors":[]},"be13fe029d3e7f89d5fd2dd79e72eb634fc70a59":{"id":"be13fe029d3e7f89d5fd2dd79e72eb634fc70a59","index":1,"content":[{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"To help give an intuition as to why twice the mean is the highest value for the average gap, we can think of the simplest case. Imagine our population now consists of just those two people who met in the street, and their total income equals $100. The average income between the two people is $50. If inequality is at its highest — so that one person has $100, and the other has $0 — the gap between them will be $100, which is twice the mean income.","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]}],"parseErrors":[]},"ff0737e17f8f87b55b2750de268ca7418a71dd49":{"id":"ff0737e17f8f87b55b2750de268ca7418a71dd49","index":3,"content":[{"type":"text","value":[{"text":"This point was championed by Anthony Atkinson, including in his well-known paper ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"children":[{"text":"On the Measurement of Inequality","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-italic"},{"text":". See Anthony B Atkinson, “On the Measurement of Inequality”. ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"children":[{"text":"Journal of Economic Theory","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-italic"},{"text":" 2, no. 3 (1970): 244–63. Available ","spanType":"span-simple-text"},{"url":"http://faculty.ucr.edu/~jorgea/econ261/atkinson_inequality.pdf","children":[{"text":"here","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"spanType":"span-link"},{"text":".","spanType":"span-simple-text"}],"parseErrors":[]}],"parseErrors":[]}}},"type":"article","title":"Measuring inequality: what is the Gini coefficient?","authors":["Joe Hasell"],"excerpt":"The Gini coefficient is the most common way of measuring inequality. But what does it actually measure? And how does it differ from other measures of inequality?","subtitle":"The Gini coefficient is the most common way of measuring inequality. But what does it actually measure? And how does it differ from other measures of inequality?","authorRoles":{},"featured-image":"what-is-the-gini-coefficient-thumbnail.png"},"contentMd5":"","createdAt":"2023-02-24T10:14:44.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-03T13:41:36.000Z","published":true,"publishedAt":"2023-06-30T07:18:00.000Z","breadcrumbs":[{"label":"Economic Inequality","href":"https://ourworldindata.org/economic-inequality"}],"manualBreadcrumbs":null,"tags":[{"id":135,"name":"Economic Inequality","slug":"economic-inequality"}],"linkedAuthors":[{"slug":"joe-hasell","name":"Joe Hasell","featuredImage":"joe-hasell.jpg","updatedAt":"2025-10-13 11:42:01"}],"linkedDocuments":{},"linkedStaticViz":{},"linkedCharts":{"gini-coefficient-wb":{"configType":"multi-dim","originalSlug":"gini-coefficient-wb","title":"Gini coefficient World Bank","dimensionSlugs":["survey_comparability"],"resolvedUrl":"https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/gini-coefficient-wb","tags":["Economic Inequality"],"archivedPageVersion":{"archivalDate":"20261019-210306","archiveUrl":"https://archive.ourworldindata.org/20261019-210306/grapher/gini-coefficient-wb.html","type":"archived-page-version"}}},"linkedNarrativeCharts":{"three-metrics-of-inequality-usa-fra-idn-ury":{"name":"three-metrics-of-inequality-usa-fra-idn-ury","title":"Proportional change in three inequality metrics","chartConfigId":"01946a74-807a-70a5-a43d-77a0772210af","parentChartSlug":"inequality-metrics-before-tax-wid","queryParamsForParentChart":{"tab":"chart","time":"1990..latest","focus":"","country":"USA~FRA~IDN~URY"},"latestArchivedParent":{"archivalDate":"20260909-232403","archiveUrl":"https://archive.ourworldindata.org/20260909-232403/grapher/inequality-metrics-before-tax-wid.html","type":"archived-page-version"}}},"linkedIndicators":{},"linkedCallouts":{},"imageMetadata":{"what-is-the-gini-coefficient-thumbnail.png":{"id":2970,"googleId":null,"filename":"what-is-the-gini-coefficient-thumbnail.png","defaultAlt":null,"extractedText":"","originalWidth":1200,"updatedAt":1739901456111,"originalHeight":630,"cloudflareId":"311c2eff-478c-44e3-d41b-271b651e7f00","hash":"0b025cd43782279fc9db07abaf1d2cbfdd1a65279bc07a806fbc7c9cbc09e783","userId":46,"replacedBy":null,"version":0},"Gini-measure-schematic-1.png":{"id":139,"googleId":"19Zjn0c4LsUEiPQGZmyM1HirxAMqyu4Ez","filename":"Gini-measure-schematic-1.png","defaultAlt":"A visual explanation of the Gini coefficient is presented in two charts. On the left, a bar chart illustrates income distribution, showing that the poorest 20% earn 5% of total income, while the richest 20% earn 50% of total income. The bars represent shares of income earned by each 20% of the population, increasing as income levels rise.\n\nOn the right, a plot demonstrates the Lorenz Curve, with the x-axis representing the cumulative share of the population (from lowest to highest income) and the y-axis showing the cumulative share of income earned. The curve illustrates the inequality in income distribution. The 'Line of Equality' is a straight line, representing perfect income equality. \n\nThe shaded area labeled 'A' between the Lorenz Curve and the Line of Equality indicates income inequality, while area 'B' represents the remaining portion under the Lorenz Curve. The formula provided states that the Gini coefficient equals A divided by the sum of A and B, serving as a measure of income inequality.\n\nData sources are credited to \"Our World in Data\" and licensed under CC-BY-SA by Max Roser.","extractedText":"Visual Explanation of the Gini Coefficient The bar chart on the left shows a simple distribution of incomes. The total population is split up in parts and ordered from the poorest to the richest . The bar chart shows how much income each part of of the income distribution earns. The chart on the right shows the same information in a different way, both axis show the cumulative shares: The poorest of the population earn of the total income, the next earn – so that the poorest of the population earn etc. The curve resulting from this way of displaying the data is called the Lorenz Curve. If there was no income inequality the resulting Lorenz Curve would be a straight line – the 'Line of Equality'. A larger area (A) between the Lorenz Curve and the Line of Equality means a higher level of inequality. The ratio of A/(A+B) is therefore a measure of inequality and is referred to as the Gini coefficient, Gini index, or simply the Gini. Gini coefficient = A / (A+B) The richest earn of all income Share of Income Earned by each of the distribution The poorest earn of all income Share of the Population (ordered from lowest to highest incomes) Cumulative Share of Income Earned Line of Equality Lorenz Curve A B Cumulative Share of the Population (ordered from lowest to highest incomes) This visualization is available at OurWorldInData.org. There you find research, visualisations and more visualizations on this topic. Licensed under CC-BY-SA by the author Max Roser. Our World in Data","originalWidth":3000,"updatedAt":1680618434819,"originalHeight":2347,"cloudflareId":"be3abb2a-40e0-4fb9-2d13-184aa2078600","hash":"19287a69115d844519bdc375ff744097da8fa8282b3e9db61aac3d2523a4efd0","userId":null,"replacedBy":null,"version":0}},"relatedCharts":[]}</script><script>
